The final match of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]], the 19th edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[association football|football]] teams, was played at [[FNB Stadium|Soccer City]] in [[Johannesburg]], South Africa, on 11 July 2010. It was contested by the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] and [[Spain national football team|Spain]], the first and only time two European teams contested a World Cup final held outside of Europe, as all other finals held outside of Europe featured a South American side. 

The tournament comprised hosts [[South Africa national football team|South Africa]] and 31 other teams who emerged from [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]]. The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]]. En route to the final, the Netherlands finished first in [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]], with three wins, after which they defeated [[Slovakia national football team|Slovakia]] in the round of 16, [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] in the quarter-final and [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] in the semi-final. Spain finished top of [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]] with two wins and one loss, before defeating [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] in the round of 16, [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] in the quarter-final and [[Germany national football team|Germany]] in the semi-final. The final took place in front of 84,490 supporters, with more than 909&nbsp;million watching on television, and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Howard Webb]] from England.

[[Sergio Ramos]] had a chance to score for Spain early in the first half when his shot was saved by Netherlands [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]] [[Maarten Stekelenburg]], while [[Arjen Robben]] had a chance for the Netherlands when he hit a low shot from the edge of the [[penalty area]] shortly before [[half-time]], which was saved by [[Iker Casillas]]. There were numerous [[Penalty card#Yellow card|booking]]s throughout the first half, which [[BBC Sport]]'s Paul Fletcher wrote had "disturbed the rhythm of the match". Robben missed an opportunity in the 62nd minute when he was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Casillas, with the goalkeeper able to intercept his attempted shot with the toe of his boot to prevent the goal. [[David Villa]] then had a chance for Spain {{convert|4|yards|m|sigfig=1}} from the Netherlands goal, but Stekelenburg saved his shot. Ramos missed a [[Header (association football)|header]] in front of goal on 77 minutes, sending the ball over the crossbar. With the match goalless after 90 minutes, it went to [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]]. [[John Heitinga]] received a second booking during extra time, which resulted in him being [[Penalty card#Red card|sent off]], and four minutes before the end, [[Andrés Iniesta]] gave Spain the lead and the title with a powerful right-footed shot from a short pass by Fabregas, into the left corner of the goal to secure a 1–0 win.

Spain's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|first World Cup title]], as well as the first World Cup win by a European team outside Europe. Iniesta was named the [[Player of the match|man of the match]], while Casillas was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Glove|Golden Glove]] as FIFA's outstanding goalkeeper of the tournament. Spain went on to win [[UEFA Euro 2012]] for their third successive trophy, but failed to defend the World Cup at [[2014 FIFA World Cup|the 2014 tournament]] in Brazil, becoming the second successive World Cup holders [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group B|to be eliminated in the group phase]] after defeats against the Netherlands and [[Chile national football team|Chile]].

==Background==
[[File:Jo'bulani.jpg|thumb|right|An example of the Adidas Jabulani/Jo'bulani (gold colored Jabulani) ball used in the match]]
The [[2010 FIFA World Cup]] was the 19th edition of the [[FIFA World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s football competition for national teams, held between 11 June and 11 July 2010 in South Africa.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite web |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |last1=Tikkanen |first1=Amy |last2=Augustyn |first2=Adam |last3=Levy |first3=Michael |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828023709/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=28 August 2021 |last4=Ray |first4=Michael |last5=Luebering |first5=J. E. |last6=Lotha |first6=Gloria |last7=Young |first7=Grace |last8=Shepherd |first8=Melinda C. |last9=Sinha |first9=Surabhi |last10=Rodriguez |first10=Emily |work=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=19 August 2021 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football}}</ref><ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2010f.html |archive-date=19 October 2018 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181019014641/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2010f.html |url-status=live}}</ref> [[South Africa national soccer team|South Africa]] qualified for the finals automatically as tournament hosts, while 205 teams competed for the remaining 31 spots through [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualifying|qualifying rounds]] organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and held between August 2007 and November 2009.<ref>{{Cite web |title=England's World Cup History by the Numbers |last=Peltier |first=Jamie |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |date=10 February 2011 |access-date=4 October 2021 |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/603867-englands-world-cup-history-by-the-numbers |archive-date=4 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211004164335/https://bleacherreport.com/articles/603867-englands-world-cup-history-by-the-numbers |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RSSSF_Qual">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010 Qualifying |last1=King |first1=Ian |last2=Lugo |first2=Erik Francisco |last3=Stokkermans |first3=Karel |last4=Zlotkowski |first4=Andre |date=12 Sep 2013 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2010q.html |archive-date=8 July 2016 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160708145326/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2010q.html |url-status=live}}</ref> In the finals, the teams were divided into eight groups of four with each team playing each other once. The two top teams from each group advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knock-out phase]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/> The defending champions from the [[2006 FIFA World Cup|2006 World Cup]] were [[Italy national football team|Italy]].<ref name="Britanica"/>

The match ball for the 2010 FIFA World Cup final, revealed on 20 April 2010, was the Jo'bulani, a gold version of the [[Adidas Jabulani]] ball used for every other match in the tournament.<ref name="jobulani">{{cite news |title=Glittering golden ball for Final |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/newsid=1196426/ |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=20 April 2010 |access-date=9 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100714134114/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/newsid%3D1196426/ |archive-date=14 July 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The name of the ball is a reference to "Jo'burg", a common nickname for [[Johannesburg]], the match venue.<ref name="jobulani"/> The gold colouring of the ball mirrors the colour of the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy]] and also echoes another of Johannesburg's nicknames: "the City of Gold".<ref name="jobulani"/> The Jo'bulani became the second ball to be specifically produced for the FIFA World Cup final, after the [[Adidas Teamgeist|Teamgeist Berlin]] was used for the [[2006 FIFA World Cup final|2006 final]].<ref name="jobulani"/>

The 2010 final was the first time since [[1978 FIFA World Cup final|1978]] that neither of the finalists had previously won the World Cup. The [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] had been runners-up twice before, losing 2–1 to [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] in [[1974 FIFA World Cup final|1974]], and 3–1 (after extra time) to [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] in 1978.<ref name="Britanica"/> Reaching the 2010 final was [[Spain national football team|Spain]]'s best performance in the World Cup, their having previously finished fourth in [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]] when the tournament had a [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin]] final stage, and having reached the quarter-final stage in [[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]], [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]], [[1994 FIFA World Cup|1994]] and [[2002 FIFA World Cup|2002]], when single-elimination knock-out stages featured.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1950 |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |last2=Jarreta |first2=Sergio Henrique |date=5 July 2017 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/50f.html |archive-date=6 May 2021 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506121643/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/50f.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Soccer: Spain World Cup factbox |publisher=[[Reuters]] |date=21 May 2018 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/soccer-worldcup-esp-factbox-idINKCN1IM1IR |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075356/https://www.reuters.com/article/soccer-worldcup-esp-factbox-idINKCN1IM1IR |url-status=live}}</ref> Spain were the reigning European champions, having won [[UEFA Euro 2008]], a tournament in which the Netherlands reached the quarter-final before being eliminated by [[Russia national football team|Russia]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/08e.html |access-date=22 July 2021 |title=European Championship 2008 |date=14 March 2013 |first=Marcel |last=Haisma |archive-date=18 April 2018 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180418141420/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/08e.html |url-status=live}}</ref> This was the first meeting between the two teams in the main tournament stages of either a World Cup or a [[UEFA European Championship|European Championship]]. In all-time head-to-head results, the teams had met nine times previously since 1920, with the Netherlands winning four games to Spain's three and one draw, all in either friendlies or European Championship qualifying games.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Netherlands national football team: record v Spain |work=11v11 |publisher=[[Association of Football Statisticians|AFS Enterprises]] |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/netherlands/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Spain/ |archive-date=27 July 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210727064418/https://www.11v11.com/teams/netherlands/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Spain/ |url-status=live}}</ref> At the start of the tournament, Spain were ranked second in the [[FIFA World Rankings]], behind Brazil, while the Netherlands were ranked fourth.<ref name="Rankings26May2010">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men?dateId=id9276 |title=Men's Ranking, 26 May 2010 |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=26 May 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075419/https://platform.twitter.com/widgets/widget_iframe.58065ae230495f5d9e4b6a916472b2c1.html?origin=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.fifa.com |url-status=live}}</ref>

