Like the Netherlands, Spain also won all of the matches in [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 5|their qualifying campaign]].<ref name="RSSSF_Qual"/> They were then drawn in [[2010 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]], alongside [[Chile national football team|Chile]], [[Honduras national football team|Honduras]] and [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Group H: Chile, Honduras, Spain, Switzerland |last=Fleming |first=Mark |work=[[The Independent]] |date=23 October 2011 |access-date=4 October 2021 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/group-h-chile-honduras-spain-switzerland-1994008.html |archive-date=4 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211004200611/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/group-h-chile-honduras-spain-switzerland-1994008.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Spain began their finals campaign on 16 June 2010 in the Moses Mabhida Stadium against Switzerland. In what ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' reporter Jeremy Wilson described as "among the bigger shocks in the competition's entire history", Switzerland won the game 1–0 with [[Gelson Fernandes]] scoring the winner in the second half after teammate [[Eren Derdiyok]] had collided with Spanish goalkeeper [[Iker Casillas]] when through on goal.<ref name="SpainSwitzTel">{{cite web |first=Jeremy |last=Wilson |title=Spain 0 Switzerland 1: match report |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=16 June 2010 |access-date=6 October 2021 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/news/7830225/Spain-0-Switzerland-1-match-report.html |url-access=registration |archive-date=6 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006133956/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/news/7830225/Spain-0-Switzerland-1-match-report.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Their second game was at Johannesburg's [[Ellis Park Stadium]], against Honduras. [[David Villa]] opened the scoring on 17 minutes when he beat two defenders and hit a shot into the top corner of the goal. He doubled the lead shortly after half-time with a shot from the edge of the penalty area following a [[Push and run|one-two]] with [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]]. Villa missed the chance for a hat-trick when his 60th-minute penalty went wide of the goalpost, and the game finished 2–0 to Spain.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010: Spain v Honduras – as it happened! |last=Glendenning |first=Barry |author-link=Barry Glendenning |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=21 June 2010 |access-date=6 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/21/world-cup-2010-spain-honduras-live |archive-date=6 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006164750/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/21/world-cup-2010-spain-honduras-live |url-status=live}}</ref> Spain entered their final game against Chile, at the [[Loftus Versfeld Stadium]] in [[Pretoria]] on 25 June, needing a win to guarantee their progression to the knockout stage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010 team news: Chile v Spain |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=25 June 2010 |access-date=6 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/25/team-news-chile-spain |archive-date=6 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006175348/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/25/team-news-chile-spain |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="GuardianMomentByMomentSpainChile">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010: Chile v Spain – as it happened |last=Smyth |first=Rob |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=25 June 2010 |access-date=6 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/25/world-cup-2010-chile-spain-live |archive-date=6 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211006175342/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/25/world-cup-2010-chile-spain-live |url-status=live}}</ref> Villa gave Spain the lead on 24 minutes, with what ''[[The Guardian]]''{{'s}} Rob Smyth described as "the goal of the World Cup so far". Chilean goalkeeper [[Claudio Bravo]] came out of the penalty area and made a [[Tackle (football move)#Association football|tackle]] on [[Fernando Torres]], but the loose ball then reached Villa on the left of the pitch and he curled a shot into the net from a distance of around {{convert|40|yards|m}}. They doubled their lead on 37 minutes when [[Andrés Iniesta]] scored following a pass from Villa.<ref name="GuardianMomentByMomentSpainChile"/> Chile's [[Marco Estrada (footballer)|Marco Estrada]] was sent off for a second [[Penalty card|yellow card]] when the referee judged that he had [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)|fouled]] Torres in the build-up to the goal. [[Rodrigo Millar]] pulled a goal back for Chile after half-time, but Spain held on for a 2–1 and a place in the next round as group winners.<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Chile 1–2 Spain |last=Whyatt |first=Chris |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 June 2010 |access-date=7 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_47/default.stm |archive-date=7 September 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170907082735/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_47/default.stm |url-status=live}}</ref>

Spain's round-of-16 game was against [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] at the Cape Town Stadium, on 29 June.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spain 1–0 Portugal |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=29 June 2010 |access-date=7 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/match-center/300061498 |archive-date=8 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008083428/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/match-center/300061498 |url-status=live}}</ref> Both teams had chances to score in the first half, but it was Spain who took the lead on 63 minutes when Xavi found Villa with a back-heeled pass who scored into the top of the goal after his initial shot was saved by Portuguese goalkeeper [[Eduardo Carvalho]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spain 1–0 Portugal |last=Bevan |first=Chris |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=29 June 2010 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_56/default.stm |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075359/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_56/default.stm |url-status=live}}</ref> Portugal's [[Ricardo Costa (footballer, born 1981)|Ricardo Costa]] was sent off with two minutes remaining, for elbowing [[Joan Capdevila]], and Spain went on to complete a 1–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010: Spain v Portugal – as it happened |last=Glendenning |first=Barry |author-link=Barry Glendenning |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=29 June 2010 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/29/world-cup-2010-spain-portugal-live |archive-date=8 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008100723/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/29/world-cup-2010-spain-portugal-live |url-status=live}}</ref> In the quarter-final, Spain faced [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] at Ellis Park Stadium on 3 July.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Paraguay 0–1 Spain |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=3 July 2010 |access-date=7 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/match-center/300061506 |archive-date=8 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008164227/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/match-center/300061506 |url-status=live}}</ref> Paraguay put the ball into the goal shortly before half-time but it was disallowed as [[Óscar Cardozo]] was ruled offside.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010: Paraguay 0–1 Spain |last=Wilson |first=Paul |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=3 July 2010 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/03/david-villa-spain-paraguay |archive-date=25 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150925040000/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/03/david-villa-spain-paraguay |url-status=live}}</ref> Paraguay were awarded a penalty on 57 minutes, taken by Cardozo after [[Gerard Piqué]] had fouled him, but it was saved by Casillas. Two minutes later, Spain were awarded a penalty of their own when [[Antolín Alcaraz]] fouled Villa. [[Xabi Alonso]] put the ball past the goalkeeper into the net, but the referee ordered it to be retaken due to Spanish players being inside the penalty area when the penalty was taken. Alonso's second attempt, the third penalty of the match, was saved by goalkeeper [[Justo Villar]], and the score remained 0–0. The deadlock was finally broken by Villa, who scored a goal on 82 minutes, which bounced off both posts before going in, to give Spain a 1–0 win.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Paraguay 0–1 Spain |last=Fletcher |first=Paul |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=3 July 2010 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_60/default.stm |archive-date=22 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201122075546/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_60/default.stm |url-status=live}}</ref> Their semi-final match took place on 7 July against Germany at the Moses Mabhida Stadium. For the third successive game, Spain won 1–0, the winner a headed goal by [[Carles Puyol]]. [[Kevin McCarra]] of ''The Guardian'' commented afterwards that these results did not imply that Spain were "grinding out" results, however. He said that their play was "enjoyable as well as masterful".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany 0–1 Spain |last=McCarra |first=Kevin |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=7 July 2010 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/07/germany-spain-world-cup-match-report |archive-date=8 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211008172823/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/07/germany-spain-world-cup-match-report |url-status=live}}</ref>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
[[File:Howard_Webb_march11.jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Howard Webb|[[Howard Webb]] was the final's referee]]

[[Howard Webb]] of England was named as the [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the final, along with fellow Englishmen Darren Cann and Michael Mullarkey who were the [[Assistant referee (association football)|assistant referees]]. Webb was the first Englishman to [[FIFA World Cup referees#World Cup Final match officials|referee a World Cup final]] since [[Jack Taylor (referee)|Jack Taylor]] officiated the 1974 final.<ref name="GuardianRefDecision">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010: England's Howard Webb to referee World Cup final |last=Fifield |first=Dominic |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=8 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/08/howard-webb-referee-world-cup-final1 |archive-date=24 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210524165620/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/08/howard-webb-referee-world-cup-final1 |url-status=live}}</ref> A former police officer from [[Rotherham]], Webb was one of the English [[Select Group]] of referees, and had officiated [[Premier League]] matches since 2003.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Howard Webb: 'Simulation is emotive. It's trying to cheat' |last=Wallace |first=Sam |work=[[The Independent]] |date=19 December 2014 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/howard-webb-i-d-be-crazy-say-i-don-t-miss-big-games-9937084.html |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075359/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/howard-webb-i-d-be-crazy-say-i-don-t-miss-big-games-9937084.html |url-status=live}}</ref> He was appointed to the FIFA list of international match referees in 2005, and before the World Cup, he had taken charge of the [[2010 UEFA Champions League Final]] and the [[2009 FA Cup Final]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=The best and worst moments of Howard Webb's refereeing career |last=Grounds |first=Benjamin |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=6 August 2014 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/aug/06/howard-webb-best-and-worst-moments-referee |archive-date=20 April 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160420014149/http://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/aug/06/howard-webb-best-and-worst-moments-referee |url-status=live}}</ref> Webb had refereed three prior games at the 2010 World Cup.<ref name="GuardianRefDecision"/> [[Yuichi Nishimura]] and Toru Sagara, both from Japan, were the fourth and fifth officials respectively.<ref name="FifaMatchOverview">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/southafrica2010/matches/round=249721/match=300061509/index.html |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=11 July 2021 |title=Netherlands 0–1 Spain |access-date=13 October 2021 |archive-date=9 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190109114920/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/southafrica2010/matches/round=249721/match=300061509/index.html |url-status=dead}}</ref>

A closing ceremony for the World Cup was held on the pitch before the game, featuring dancers in the colours of the 32 tournament participants.<ref name="Nelson1">{{Cite web |title=Nelson Mandela attends World Cup closing ceremony |last=Batty |first=David |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/jul/11/nelson-mandela-world-cup-final |archive-date=24 July 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130724041644/http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2010/jul/11/nelson-mandela-world-cup-final |url-status=live}}</ref> There were also dancers dressed as elephants and hippopotamuses gathered around a [[Geologic depression|watering hole]], denoting South Africa's wildlife, and others in clothing typical of the country's urban youth. Video highlights from the tournament were projected on to the pitch, while local music group [[Ladysmith Black Mambazo]] performed their song "Rain Rain Beautiful Rain" and [[Shakira]] sang the World Cup anthem "[[Waka Waka (This Time for Africa)]]".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Soccer City erupts in light for World Cup closer |agency=Agencies |work=[[The Economic Times]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/soccer-city-erupts-in-light-for-world-cup-closer/articleshow/6156326.cms?from=mdr |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075359/https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/soccer-city-erupts-in-light-for-world-cup-closer/articleshow/6156326.cms?from=mdr |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Nelson Mandela]], who had missed the opening ceremony due to the death of his great-granddaughter in a road accident, made an appearance on the pitch in his wheelchair before the game, alongside wife [[Graça Machel]]. He was greeted with a noisy standing ovation and a peal of [[vuvuzela]]s from the spectators at the game.<ref name="DreamFinale">{{Cite web |title=Nelson Mandela gives World Cup a dream finale with a wave and a smile |last=Smith |first=David |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/11/world-cup-final-nelson-mandela |archive-date=25 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210525100902/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/11/world-cup-final-nelson-mandela |url-status=live}}</ref> Other attendees at the match included members of both the [[Monarchy of the Netherlands|Dutch]]<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ad.nl/ad/nl/1049/Oranje/article/detail/496373/2010/07/08/Willem-Alexander-en-Maxima-bij-WK-finale.dhtml |title=Willem Alexander en Máxima bij WK-finale |work=[[Algemeen Dagblad]] |language=nl|access-date=10 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100713031511/http://www.ad.nl/ad/nl/1049/Oranje/article/detail/496373/2010/07/08/Willem-Alexander-en-Maxima-bij-WK-finale.dhtml |archive-date=13 July 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Spanish Royal Family|Spanish]] royal families. South African dignitaries and celebrities attended, including [[Charlize Theron]] and South African president [[Jacob Zuma]].<ref name="DreamFinale"/><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.marca.com/2010/07/11/futbol/mundial_2010/1278861299.html |title=Mandela estará en el Soccer City pero no se quedará a ver la final |work=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]] |language=es|access-date=11 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100713004929/http://www.marca.com/2010/07/11/futbol/mundial_2010/1278861299.html |archive-date=13 July 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref> Spaniards [[Plácido Domingo]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Domingo: Proud to be Spanish |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=19 July 2010 |access-date=16 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/news/domingo-proud-spanish-1275610 |archive-date=16 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211016141230/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/news/domingo-proud-spanish-1275610 |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Rafael Nadal]] and [[Pau Gasol]]<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.marca.com/2010/07/10/futbol/mundial_2010/selecciones/espana/1278763149.html |title=Apoyo de lujo para La Roja en la gran final |work=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]] |language=es|access-date=10 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100713004715/http://www.marca.com/2010/07/10/futbol/mundial_2010/selecciones/espana/1278763149.html |archive-date=13 July 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref> were in attendance to cheer on their team. American actor [[Morgan Freeman]], who had played Mandela in the 2009 film ''[[Invictus (film)|Invictus]]'', was also present at the game.<ref name="DreamFinale"/>

===First half===
[[File:FIFA World Cup 2010 Final Line-ups.jpg|thumb|left|South African president [[Jacob Zuma]] and other dignitaries shaking hands with the lined-up teams before kick-off.]]
The Netherlands [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] at approximately 8:30&nbsp;pm local time (6:30&nbsp;pm [[UTC]]) in temperatures of {{convert|14|°C|°F|abbr=on}}, with humidity of 34%.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute">{{Cite web |title=World Cup final: Holland v Spain – as it happened |last=Murray |first=Scott |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=9 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/11/holland-spain-world-cup-final-live |archive-date=8 August 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210808074212/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/11/holland-spain-world-cup-final-live |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ned-esp_line-ups"/> Spain won a [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] on 5 minutes, when van Bronckhorst fouled Alonso.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Xavi's kick reached [[Sergio Ramos]] in the penalty area, who headed the ball towards the bottom-left corner of the goal, but Stekelenburg was able to dive down to make the [[Glossary of association football terms#S|save]].<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="UEFAMatchReport">{{Cite web |title=Iniesta earns Spain World Cup crown |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=9 October 2021 |url=https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/0253-0d7c93af1ed8-28be4efeaa14-1000--iniesta-earns-spain-world-cup-crown/ |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075400/https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/0253-0d7c93af1ed8-28be4efeaa14-1000--iniesta-earns-spain-world-cup-crown/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Pique then gained possession of the loose ball, and attempted to pass to Villa in the centre, but Stekelenburg intercepted.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute">{{cite web |first=Chris |last=Murphy |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/commentary?gameId=264123 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |title=Netherlands vs. Spain – Football Match Commentary |access-date=11 October 2021 |date=11 July 2010 |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075438/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/commentary?gameId=264123 |url-status=live}}</ref> Two minutes later, Kuyt obtained the ball following an error by Alonso, and took a shot at the Spanish goal from {{convert|25|yards|m}} out but it was weak and Casillas was able to claim the ball. Spain had a chance on 11 minutes when Ramos beat Kuyt after receiving the ball from Iniesta, but [[John Heitinga]] deflected his shot over the crossbar. Alonso took the resulting corner, which was [[Volley (association football)|volleyed]] by Villa into the [[Glossary of association football terms#S|side netting]]. On 15 minutes, Van Persie received the first [[Penalty card#Yellow card|booking]] of the match for a foul on Capdevila, with Puyol also being shown the yellow card two minutes later for a high tackle on Robben. Sneijder hit a {{convert|25|yards|m|adj=on}} shot at goal from the resulting free kick which was caught by Casillas.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/>

On 22 minutes, [[Mark van Bommel]] was booked for a foul during a sliding tackle on Iniesta, with Ramos joining him in the book two minutes later for bringing down Kuyt. [[Nigel de Jong]] then became the fifth player of the match to be booked with a high tackle on Alonso which resulted in his studs connecting with the Spaniard's chest.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Busquets complained to the referee that this should have resulted in a sending off,<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> Paul Fletcher of [[BBC Sport]] described the multiple bookings as having "disturbed the rhythm of the match", but noted that "several crude challenges left him with little option".<ref name="BBCSportFinalReport">{{Cite web |title=Netherlands 0–1 Spain (aet) |last=Fletcher |first=Paul |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_64/default.stm |quote=brutal disappointment |archive-date=10 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170810004846/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_64/default.stm |url-status=live}}</ref> Casillas collided with and injured Puyol on 33 minutes, putting the ball out of play so that he could receive treatment. The Netherlands attempted to return the ball to Casillas from the resulting [[throw-in]], but the bounce off the pitch surprised Casillas and he had to push the ball behind to prevent a Netherlands goal. The Netherlands returned the ball to him from the [[Corner kick|corner]].<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Netherlands had two opportunities to score from a corner on 37 minutes, but van Bommel and then Mathijsen both failed to connect properly with their attempted shots. [[Pedro (footballer, born 1987)|Pedro]] then ran upfield with the ball but his shot went wide of the goal.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> Shortly before half-time, Robben received the ball on the edge of the Spanish penalty area and his low shot towards the corner of the goal was saved by Casillas as the half ended 0–0.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

===Second half===
There were no [[Substitution (sport)|substitutions]] at half-time, and Spain kicked off the second half.<ref name="FifaMatchOverview"/> They had the first opportunity of the half on 48 minutes, when the ball reached Capdevila around {{convert|6|yards|m|sigfig=1}} from goal, but he scuffed his attempted side-footed shot. Spain had a penalty appeal turned down on 50 minutes when van Bommel and Alonso were contesting the ball, after which the Netherlands moved down the field and crossed the ball into the Spanish penalty area, but no Netherlands players were able to reach the ball.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> A mix-up between Casillas and Puyol on 52 minutes led to a Netherlands throw-in, from which they built an attack, but it ended when van Persie's shot was claimed by Casillas. Spain then won a free kick around {{convert|25|yards|m}} from goal, following a foul by van Bronckhorst for which he was booked. It was taken by Xavi, but went wide of the goal.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> On 56 minutes, Heitinga was booked for a foul on Villa and two minutes later the Netherlands won a free kick when Iniesta fouled Robben. The ball reached Heitinga in the penalty area, but he missed his shot although he was deemed to be offside.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Spain made the first substitution of the match on 60 minutes, with [[Jesús Navas]] coming on in place of Pedro.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/>

In the 62nd minute, Robben had what [[ESPN]]'s Elko Born later described as "the most obvious opportunity to score during regular time".<ref name="ESPNRobbenChance">{{cite web |title=Arjen Robben and the trauma of 2010 |publisher=[[ESPN]] |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/club/name/449/blog/post/1868716/headline |access-date=13 October 2021 |date=8 June 2014 |first=Elko |last=Born |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075400/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/club/name/449/blog/post/1868716/headline |url-status=live}}</ref> Receiving the ball from a Sneijder pass upfield, Robben was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Casillas.<ref name="BBCSportFinalReport"/> He delayed his shot before attempting to flick the ball over the goalkeeper into the corner of the goal, but his shot lacked height and Casillas was able to put the ball out for a corner which the Dutch failed to score from.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNRobbenChance"/> On 67 minutes, Capdevila was booked for a foul on van Persie, and three minutes later Spain had an opportunity when Villa received the ball {{convert|4|yards|m|sigfig=1}} from goal following a pass by Navas. His shot was parried behind by Stekelenburg, in what ''The Guardian''{{'s}} Scott Murray described as an "amazing save, terrible miss".<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> The Netherlands made a change on 71 minutes, as Elia replaced Kuyt, before Spain won a free kick around {{convert|25|yards|m}} from goal which Villa kicked wide. Spain had another chance a minute later as Alonso found Navas, who sent in a cross, but Villa missed with his attempted volley.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> On 77 minutes, Spain won a corner when Villa's shot was saved following a one-two with Xavi. The ball reached Ramos from the corner, who was [[Marking (association football)|unmarked]] {{convert|6|yards|m|sigfig=1}} from goal, but his header went over the crossbar.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="BBCSportFinalReport"/> Robben then had a chance on 83 minutes, when he beat Puyol and attempted to take the ball around Casillas, but the goalkeeper dived low to take the ball from him before he could do so. Robben was booked for dissent, saying that Puyol had fouled him, and the game went to extra time, finishing 0–0 at the end of 90 minutes.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/>

===Extra time===
Spain appealed for a penalty two minutes into the extra period, when Xavi went down following a clash with Heitinga, but it was not given. Three minutes later, [[Cesc Fàbregas]], who had come on as a substitute for Alonso shortly before the end of regular time, received a pass from Iniesta and was one-on-one with the goalkeeper. He directed a shot low shot towards the corner of the goal, but Stekelenburg was able to claim the ball. This was followed by an opportunity for the Netherlands when Fàbregas impeded Casillas following a Netherlands corner. The ball reached Mathijsen, who was not marked but his attempt to head the ball into the goal from {{convert|6|yards|m|sigfig=1}} went over the crossbar.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Spain then had two chances, first through Puyol, whose attempted header towards goal lacked power and direction, then through Iniesta who ran forward after receiving a pass from Fàbregas, but lost the ball to van Bronckhorst without being able to shoot or pass the ball to Navas, who was in space. The Netherlands then made their second change, bringing on van der Vaart for de Jong.<ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> On 101 minutes, Navas ran with the ball down the right-hand flank, following passes by Fàbregas and Villa, before hitting his shot into the side netting. Many spectators at the ground thought it had been a goal. Three minutes later, Fàbregas fired a shot which went just wide of the goalpost.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/>

The Netherlands made a substitution in the 105th minute, bringing on [[Edson Braafheid]] for van Bronckhorst. After the extra-time interval, Spain substituted Fernando Torres for Villa.<ref name="FifaMatchOverview"/> Braafheid made his first touch when defending a Xavi cross, the ball bouncing off the top of his head and into Stekelenburg's arms after he had turned away from it.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/> Xavi then found Iniesta on the left of the field, but his attempted run into the penalty area was stopped when he was brought down by Heitinga. The referee gave Heitinga a second booking, which resulted in him being sent off, the fifth player to be dismissed in a World Cup final.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Another booking followed for van der Wiel on 111 minutes when he fouled Iniesta, and Stekelenburg punched the ball clear from the resulting free kick before Navas hit a shot which went high into the stand behind the goal. On 115 minutes, the Netherlands won a free kick when Pique fouled Elia, which was taken by Sneijder. It deflected off the Spanish [[Glossary of association football terms#W|wall]] and Casillas fingertipped it behind, but the referee gave a [[goal kick]] instead of a corner. Spain then broke upfield through Torres, who passed into the centre where the ball bounced off a Netherlands defender. Fàbregas retrieved it and passed to Iniesta who was in space on the right-hand side of the penalty area. He took one touch with his right foot before striking the ball with his right foot on the half-volley past Stekelenburg into the left corner of the goal to give Spain the lead four minutes before the end. The Netherlands' players complained to the assistant referee about both the corner decision and what they believed was a foul on Elia that was not given. Mathijsen received a booking for throwing the ball into the pitch in anger while Iniesta was also booked for removing his shirt while celebrating his goal. Xavi was booked on 120 minutes for kicking the ball away and Torres went down in injury time with a hamstring issue but Spain held on to win the game 1–0 and win their first World Cup.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="ESPNMinuteByMinute"/><ref name="Sky">{{cite news |first=James |last=Dall |title=World domination for Spain |url=http://www.skysports.com/football/world-cup-2010/match-report/0,28360,12097_3294798,00.html |work=Sky Sports |publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=11 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100712231653/http://www.skysports.com/football/world-cup-2010/match-report/0%2C28360%2C12097_3294798%2C00.html |archive-date=12 July 2010 |url-status=live}}</ref>

