==Post-match==
[[File:FIFA World Cup 2010 Spain with cup.jpg|thumb|Manager [[Vicente del Bosque]] lifting the trophy with the Spanish players]]
The win was Spain's first World Cup title, and it lifted them to first place in the FIFA World Rankings with the Netherlands moving into second.<ref name="Rankings14July2010">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men?dateId=id9325 |title=Men's Ranking, 14 July 2010 |publisher=[[FIFA]] |date=14 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075400/https://www.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men?dateId=id9325 |url-status=live}}</ref> Spain also became the first European nation to win a World Cup outside of Europe.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spain and the Netherlands to make history |agency=[[Deutsche Presse-Agentur]] |work=[[The Hindu]] |date=9 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.thehindu.com/sport/football/Spain-and-the-Netherlands-to-make-history/article16190469.ece |archive-date=14 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211014075425/https://www.thehindu.com/sport/football/Spain-and-the-Netherlands-to-make-history/article16190469.ece |url-status=live}}</ref> At the conclusion of the match, the Spanish team changed into their red-shirted home [[Kit (association football)|kit]] for the presentation. These shirts already had a star over the emblem, signifying their World Cup victory.<ref>{{cite web |title=World Cup Live: Netherlands vs. Spain |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/07/11/world-cup-live-netherlands-vs-spain/ |date=11 July 2010 |first=Jeff Z. |last=Klein |access-date=16 October 2021 |archive-date=13 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100713204005/https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2010/07/11/world-cup-live-netherlands-vs-spain/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Spain players formed a guard of honour for the Netherlands team as they went up to the stands to receive their runners-up medals. Afterwards, the red-shirted Spaniards went up to receive their medals, led by Xavi. Spain [[Captain (association football)|captain]] Casillas (who per tradition went last) was presented with the trophy by Zuma and FIFA president [[Sepp Blatter]].<ref name="2010WCFreviewFIFA">{{cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aKSHgMqCwbQ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130413163026/http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aKSHgMqCwbQ&gl=US&hl=en |archive-date=13 April 2013 |url-status=live |title=2010 WORLD CUP FINAL: Netherlands 0–1 Spain (AET) |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=11 July 2012 |accessdate=28 January 2023 |type=YouTube video}}</ref> As Casillas raised the trophy, a short version of the tournament's official anthem "[[Sign of a Victory]]" was played.<ref name="2010WCFreviewFIFA"/> Iniesta was named the [[player of the match|man of the match]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Andres Iniesta |last=Rollin |first=Jack |work=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=16 September 2021 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Andres-Iniesta |archive-date=14 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200814034055/https://www.britannica.com/biography/Andres-Iniesta |url-status=live}}</ref> while Casillas won the [[FIFA World Cup Golden Glove|Golden Glove]] from FIFA for the tournament's best goalkeeper and the Spanish team won the [[FIFA World Cup Fair Play Trophy|Fair Play Trophy]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=2010 FIFA World Cup South Africa: Spain – winner |publisher=[[FIFA]] |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/teams/43969 |archive-date=10 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211010091948/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2010south-africa/teams/43969 |url-status=live}}</ref>

With fourteen bookings during the match – nine to the Netherlands and five to Spain – the match set the record for the most bookings in a World Cup final, more than doubling the previous record of six from the 1986 final between West Germany and Argentina.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Most yellow cards issued in a FIFA World Cup final |work=[[Guinness World Records]] |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/most-yellow-cards-issued-in-a-fifa-world-cup-championship-match |archive-date=27 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190527012414/http://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/most-yellow-cards-issued-in-a-fifa-world-cup-championship-match |url-status=live}}</ref> The Netherlands players were critical of referee Webb, questioning why Heitinga was sent off for a second bookable offence, while Iniesta was not.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup final: Howard Webb under fire but Johan Cruyff blames Dutch |last1=Fifield |first1=Dominic |last2=Gibson |first2=Owen |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=12 July 2010 |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/12/howard-webb-world-cup-final-2010 |archive-date=3 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210303100558/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/12/howard-webb-world-cup-final-2010 |url-status=live}}</ref> Former Netherlands player [[Johan Cruyff]] was critical of his compatriots, however, saying that they had played "in a very dirty fashion", describing their contribution to the final as "ugly", "vulgar" and "[[anti-football]]". He added that the Netherlands should have had two players ([[Mark van Bommel]] and [[Nigel de Jong]]) sent off early in the match, and was critical of Webb for being too lenient on them.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/808405/ce/uk/?campaign=rss&source=soccernet&cc=5901&ver=us |title=2010 World Cup: Johan Cruyff disgusted by Netherlands' approach |publisher=[[ESPN]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=15 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100715081255/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/808405/ce/uk/?campaign=rss&source=soccernet&cc=5901&ver=us |archive-date=15 July 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The [[Associated Press]] was of the opinion that the Netherlands had "turned far too often to dirty tactics".<ref>{{cite news |title=Third time's no charm for Dutch |url=http://search.japantimes.co.jp/cgi-bin/sw20100714a2.html |work=[[The Japan Times]] |date=14 July 2010 |access-date=19 July 2010 |archive-date=13 July 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120713185500/http://www.japantimes.co.jp/text/sw20100714a2.html%23.UABu9Kgo9Rw |url-status=live}}</ref> Webb himself said, in a subsequent interview, that "Having seen [the De Jong challenge] again from my armchair, I would red-card him. The trouble in the actual game was that I had a poor view of that particular incident".<ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=I should have shown Nigel de Jong a red card, says Howard Webb |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/aug/25/howard-webb-world-cup-final |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=25 August 2010 |access-date=23 November 2010 |archive-date=10 March 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130310012003/http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2010/aug/25/howard-webb-world-cup-final |url-status=live}}</ref>

The Netherlands team were welcomed back to Amsterdam by an estimated 700,000 supporters lining the banks of the canals,<ref name="Reuters">{{Cite web |title=Dutch team get canal parade despite Cup loss |publisher=[[Reuters]] |date=13 July 2010 |access-date=16 October 2021 |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/ozasp-soccer-world-dutch-20100713-idAFJOE66C0QL20100713 |archive-date=16 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211016163204/https://www.reuters.com/article/ozasp-soccer-world-dutch-20100713-idAFJOE66C0QL20100713 |url-status=live }}</ref> and team captain van Bronckhorst and coach [[Bert van Marwijk]] were named Knights in the [[Order of Orange-Nassau]] by [[Beatrix of the Netherlands|Queen Beatrix]].<ref name="Reuters"/> Further, there were also reports that noted the play-acting and fouls by some of the Spanish players.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Spain must wait for place in pantheon |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/columns/story?id=808260&cc=3436&ver=global |publisher=[[ESPN]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=15 July 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100715220412/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/columns/story?id=808260&cc=3436&ver=global |archive-date=15 July 2010}}</ref> German footballer [[Franz Beckenbauer]], who had won the World Cup as both a player and a manager, criticised the two teams and Webb, saying that the match was "lacking flow, [with] constant protests from the players and a referee who didn't have too much of an overview".<ref>{{cite news |title=Franz Beckenbauer Blasts Spain And Netherlands For Football "Anti-Advertisement" |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/1863/world-cup-2010/2010/07/13/2023511/franz-beckenbauer-blasts-spain-and-netherlands-for-football-anti- |website=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |date=13 July 2010 |access-date=15 July 2010 |archive-date=16 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110816081840/http://www.goal.com/en/news/1863/world-cup-2010/2010/07/13/2023511/franz-beckenbauer-blasts-spain-and-netherlands-for-football-anti- |url-status=live}}</ref> Spain's ball-possession strategy in the World Cup final received mixed reactions. While some maintained that it was effective, but "boring",<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spain's win down to possession, not goals |url=http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,,5784233,00.html |publisher=[[Deutsche Welle]] |date=12 July 2010 |access-date=25 July 2010 |archive-date=17 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110817192605/http://www.dw-world.de/dw/article/0,,5784233,00.html |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=2010 FIFA World Cup Final: How Spain Won It |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/419690-2010-fifa-world-cup-final-how-spain-won-it |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |date=13 July 2010 |access-date=25 July 2010 |archive-date=18 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131218133353/http://bleacherreport.com/articles/419690-2010-fifa-world-cup-final-how-spain-won-it |url-status=live}}</ref> others claimed it was "beautiful".<ref>{{Cite news |title=World Cup 2010: Anti-Football's Defeat Brings Hope To Beautiful Game |url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/420509-world-cup-2010-anti-footballs-defeat-brings-hope-to-beautiful-game |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |date=15 July 2010 |access-date=30 July 2010 |archive-date=18 December 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131218133355/http://bleacherreport.com/articles/420509-world-cup-2010-anti-footballs-defeat-brings-hope-to-beautiful-game |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=World Cup 2010: A win for the beautiful game |url=http://www.todayonline.com/WorldCup/EDC100713-0000133/A-win-for-the-beautiful-game |work=[[Today (Singapore newspaper)|Today]] |date=13 July 2010 |access-date=30 July 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100716014326/http://www.todayonline.com/WorldCup/EDC100713-0000133/A-win-for-the-beautiful-game |archive-date=16 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Football wins as Spain lifts World Cup |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/news?slug=ap-wcup-johnleicester-110710 |publisher=[[Yahoo!]] |date=11 July 2010 |access-date=30 July 2010 |archive-date=1 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110501060231/http://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/news?slug=ap-wcup-johnleicester-110710 |url-status=live}}</ref>

FIFA estimated that 910&nbsp;million viewers worldwide watched at least part of the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Almost half the world tuned in at home to watch 2010 FIFA World Cup South Africa |url=https://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=1473143/ |date=11 July 2011 |access-date=30 May 2014 |publisher=[[FIFA]] |archive-date=31 May 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140531090758/http://www.fifa.com/newscentre/news/newsid=1473143/ |url-status=dead}}</ref> In Spain, the final attracted 15.6&nbsp;million total Spanish viewers across three networks, which represents 86% share of the audience, becoming the highest rated TV broadcast in Spanish history.<ref name="2010 FIFA World Cup Draws Record Ratings in USA, Europe, and Beyond">{{cite web |first=Todd |last=Lamansky |title=2010 FIFA World Cup Draws Record Ratings in USA, Europe, and Beyond |url=http://bleacherreport.com/articles/419916-2010-fifa-world-cup-draws-record-ratings-in-usa-europe-and-beyond#page/3 |publisher=[[Bleacher Report]] |date=13 July 2010 |access-date=20 July 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100717005824/http://bleacherreport.com/articles/419916-2010-fifa-world-cup-draws-record-ratings-in-usa-europe-and-beyond |archive-date=17 July 2010 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Spain's previous record was set by the Euro 2008 quarter-final [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] between Spain and Italy, which drew 14.1&nbsp;million viewers.<ref name="2010 FIFA World Cup Draws Record Ratings in USA, Europe, and Beyond"/> In the Netherlands, 12.2&nbsp;million people watched the final on television,<ref>{{cite web |first=Allister |last=Heath |title=World Cup 2010: a view from the Netherlands |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=14 July 2010 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/expat/expatlife/7887251/World-Cup-2010-a-view-from-the-Netherlands.html |access-date=13 October 2021 |archive-date=9 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210309102759/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/expat/expatlife/7887251/World-Cup-2010-a-view-from-the-Netherlands.html |url-status=live}}</ref> which was an estimated 74% of the total population of the country.{{efn|The estimated population in 2010 was 16.58&nbsp;million.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Population counter: How many people live in the Netherlands? |publisher=[[Statistics Netherlands]] |access-date=13 October 2021 |url=https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/visualisations/dashboard-population/population-counter |archive-date=30 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210930062008/https://www.cbs.nl/en-gb/visualisations/dashboard-population/population-counter |url-status=live}}</ref>}}

Spain's next major tournament was [[UEFA Euro 2012]], which they also won, beating Italy 4–0 in [[UEFA Euro 2012 Final|the final]] to complete three consecutive major tournament wins.<ref name="BBCThreeInaRow">{{Cite web |title=Are Spain the best team of all time? |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=2 July 2012 |access-date=20 July 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18669029 |archive-date=3 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201203142901/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18669029 |url-status=live}}</ref> At the subsequent 2014 World Cup in Brazil, Spain and Netherlands were both drawn in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group B|Group B]], from which Spain failed to advance, finishing third in the group behind the Netherlands and Chile, as well as being beaten by the Netherlands 5–1 in the rematch from four years prior and Chile 2–0. The Netherlands went on to reach the semi-final, where they lost in a penalty shoot-out to Argentina, while Chile were eliminated in the Round of 16.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2018 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=9 August 2018 |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2018f.html |archive-date=30 June 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200630070249/http://rsssf.com/tables/2018f.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

